{
  "slug": "alien-dictionary",
  "title": "Alien Dictionary",
  "topic": "graphs",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "topological sort (Kahn) over a graph built from adjacent comparisons",
  "statement": "An alien language uses the same 26 lowercase letters as English, but in an unknown order. You are handed a dictionary of its words, already sorted into that unknown order.\n\nRecover the relative order of the letters that occur anywhere in the dictionary and return it as a string holding each of those letters exactly once. For any two words that are adjacent in the list, find the first position where they disagree: the letter of the earlier word must appear before the letter of the later word in your string.\n\nEqual words, and adjacent pairs where the earlier word is a prefix of the later one, add no requirement. A word that comes before a shorter word which is its own prefix makes the dictionary impossible, since that shorter word would have had to come first; so does a set of requirements that forms a cycle, where some letter would have to come both before and after another letter.\n\nSeveral strings may satisfy the requirements. Return the lexicographically smallest one, comparing candidates as ordinary strings over that same letter set. Return the empty string \"\" when no string can satisfy them.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= words.length <= 100` and `1 <= words[i].length <= 20`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "words = [\"d\"]",
      "output": "\"d\"",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "words = [\"jhm\"]",
      "output": "\"hjm\"",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(words: string[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Only neighbouring words carry information: the first position where two adjacent words differ is one ordered pair of letters.",
    "Those pairs are directed edges; a valid alphabet is a topological order of that graph, and a cycle means the empty string.",
    "To make the answer unique, always emit the smallest letter whose in-degree is currently 0, which is exactly what a min-heap over the frontier gives you, and remember to reject a longer word that precedes its own prefix."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Both versions first turn the dictionary into a directed graph over the V distinct letters: for each adjacent pair, skip the common prefix, and when the first difference is found record an edge from the earlier letter to the later one, deduplicating so that in-degrees stay honest. A pair where one word is a proper prefix of the other and the longer word came first is an immediate contradiction. What is left is a topological order, and the lexicographically smallest one is the order produced by always emitting the smallest letter whose in-degree has dropped to 0. The brute force finds that letter by scanning the sorted letter list on every step, which is O(V) per emission; the optimal keeps the in-degree-zero frontier in a binary min-heap, so extraction costs O(log V).",
    "why_optimal": "Every adjacent pair has to be inspected to build E edges, and every one of the V letters is emitted once, so O(V + E) is the floor. The only avoidable work is finding the smallest available letter, and replacing the O(V) linear scan with a heap makes it O(log V) per extraction, giving O((V + E) log V). The constraints keep V at 26, so the practical difference is small, but the heap version is the one that does not degrade when V grows. Space is O(V + E) in both, since the adjacency sets plus in-degrees are the same data.",
    "pitfalls": "Adding the same edge twice from repeated word pairs, which inflates a letter's in-degree so it never reaches zero and a solvable dictionary is reported as impossible. Returning only the letters in the reachable part of a cycle-free graph while dropping the isolated ones, which happens if the output is built from the queue alone instead of from all distinct letters. Missing the prefix contradiction, for example [\"abc\", \"ab\"]. Assuming the answer must contain all 26 letters rather than just the letters that occur. Sorting candidates with the ordinary string compare of the whole permutation when the tie-break actually has to be decided letter by letter at the moment of emission."
  },
